# Former Gods, Part 3
Tamil title: மாஜி கடவுள்கள் - பகுதி 3

Hera was Zeus’s sister, golden, majestic, and beautiful enough to inspire folded hands. Homer called her “ox-eyed.” Zeus nevertheless made his sister his wife, and they reigned as god and goddess.

## The Goddess Hera

Perhaps Homer called her ox-eyed because she was fierce in condemning her husband’s lust and tormenting those who yielded to it. Hera possessed a golden throne and regarded the cow, peacock, and cuckoo as sacred.

Their wedding was magnificent. Gods danced in the bridal garden, and earth supposedly produced a marvellous tree bearing golden apples. Sister became wife: a divine sport. Today ordinary people recoil and call this a corrupt invention, but then reverent acceptance and recitation marked theism. A critic could be denounced by priests to kings as provoking the goddess’s wrath and executed before atheism spread.

Though Hera ruled the cosmos beside her brother-husband, distress followed because Zeus desired every beauty in heaven or earth. Rules, justice, and propriety supposedly bound ordinary people, not the primal god. Like Krishna’s erotic play with gopis, Radha, Rukmini, and others, Zeus’s affairs produced many calamities.

Hera watched him constantly, angry and jealous that another woman might boast herself more beautiful. When Zeus desired Io, daughter of the river-god Inachus, Hera descended in fury. Zeus raised black clouds as a screen and transformed Io into a heifer. Asked what it was, he claimed he had just created it. Suspecting deceit, Hera requested the animal as a gift, and Zeus had to agree.

## The Maiden Changed into a Cow

Hera entrusted Io to Argus beside a river. Greek mythology gave this lesser god eyes all over his body. He closed only two at a time, so even asleep he watched. Io could not escape and Zeus could not approach.

Io’s grieving father wandered in search of her. At the pasture she recognized him but could not speak, so the cow scratched “Io” in the ground with her hoof. Today even our people may call this absurd, but a Greek critic then became an atheist. Here similarly irrational divine tales still provoke rage against critics. Question Murugan becoming hunter, old man, tree, and summoner of an elephant to pursue Valli, and devotees still threaten the doubter.

No one in enlightened countries now believes a woman became a cow yet retained literacy. What was once theism became intellectual emptiness. Argus drove away Io’s sobbing father.

Zeus sent Hermes to kill Argus. Hermes’s enchanting flute failed because Argus’s many eyes remained open, so he told a captivating story until all closed, then killed him and freed Io. He could not restore her human form. Hera placed Argus’s eyes upon her peacock’s tail and sent a poisonous insect to torment the cow. Io fled across sea to Egypt. Zeus finally begged Hera, who restored Io as a woman but not as his lover. Devotees praised Hera’s prowess with special rites for ages.

Hera similarly ruined Zeus’s affair with Callisto, a beautiful Arcadian companion of Diana. Seeing her asleep, Zeus was seized by lust. Do not imagine he declared love and married her. He assumed Diana’s form, woke and caressed the confused woman, and overpowered her despite resistance. The meadow became his bed. He then left for other duties while the violated woman concealed the “divine secret.”

When Diana and her companions later went bathing, Callisto refused to undress. They stripped her and discovered her pregnancy. Diana condemned and expelled her, punishing her for Zeus’s outrage. After Callisto bore a son, Hera transformed her into a she-bear. A male god destroyed her chastity; a female god destroyed her form. Her only offence was beauty.

Zeus’s affairs were countless. Despite Hera’s vigilance, he became cloud, bull, or any form needed. Priests said omnipotence meant God could perform what humans could not. Humans themselves condemned theft, murder, and sexual misconduct, but mythology praised gods for such acts. Devotion shattered thought and priests kept the eye of reason closed.

An oracle told King Acrisius of Argos that his daughter Danae’s son would kill him. He imprisoned the unmarried woman underground in a brass chamber with one door and an old guard. Zeus learned of the beauty and entered as a shower of gold. Danae bore Perseus. The king enclosed mother and child in a chest and cast them into the sea, but Zeus calmed the waters and a fisherman rescued them.

Greek mythology called the rainbow Hera’s messenger; science now explains it. Here people still point to rounded stones as Krishna’s butter or worn stones as places Draupadi ground turmeric.

Hera, called Juno by Romans, once had enormous influence and temples at Argos, Sparta, and Mycenae. Today she has no shrine, lamp, festival, or fasting devotee. Knowledge made Hera a former goddess, while Mari still rules here.

28-5-49

Poseidon, younger brother of Zeus in the unenlightened heavens, ruled sea and guarded wind from a great palace. He bore a long beard and sharp trident and rode a chariot whose horses had golden manes and jewel-like hooves.

## Poseidon

Before humanity understood sky, earth, and sea, they inspired fear and wonder. Even modern people still say, “Who knows what this marvel is?” Knowledge advances by people who refuse to surrender before the unexplained, some sacrificing comfort and enduring repeated failure until fearsome mysteries become intelligible.

An unexplained object yields no benefit. Seeing something curved and shining in a dark corner, people wonder whether to approach and whether it is dangerous. Until its nature is known, its use remains unknown. A piece of sandalwood thrown in a cowshed brings no benefit to an ignorant owner. A selfish visitor may call it firewood, take it, and later return a little sandal paste while hiding its value. Ignorance creates loss, which selfish knowledge exploits, as colonizers exchanged trinkets for African diamonds.

A selfless knower would explain the sandalwood. But when most people could not identify the curved shining object, deceivers exploited uncertainty. One might call it a divine serpent, order the owner to close his eyes in prayer, then reveal an iron ring as the god’s transformed shape, build a serpent temple, and become its first priest. Humanity’s uninformed age produced countless wonders, shrines, and priesthoods in this way.

If ignorance of a ring caused such fear and servitude, how much greater must ancient fear of sky, earth, and sea have been? Research has since revealed much of nature and yielded vast benefit.

Poseidon was imagined as lord of sea and wind and one of the primal protectors of the cosmos. Greeks worshipped him in fear because commerce required long sea voyages. Each encounter with the ocean raised questions: how vast is it, where does it begin and end, what causes waves, was all this water supplied by rain, and...

---NOTES---
Part 3 begins directly with Hera and does not visibly inherit a sentence fragment from Part 2. It ends mid-question after `மழையினாலேயே நிரம்பிற்றோ?`, continued by the rainwater question at the start of `maji_kadavulgal4.md`.

Conventional English names are used for source forms including `ஹீரா` (Hera), `ஜுவஸ்` (Zeus), `அயோ` (Io), `இனாகஸ்` (Inachus), `ஆர்கஸ்` (Argus), `ஹெர்மிஸ்` (Hermes), `காலிஸ்ட்டா` (Callisto), `டயானா` (Diana), `டானே` (Danae), `பெர்ஷியஸ்` (Perseus), and `பாசிடன்` (Poseidon). The Tamil OCR remains verbatim.

The source’s Danae passage does not name Acrisius; the conventional identification is used only to clarify the English narrative. Several classical episodes are compressed or differ from standard modern retellings, and the translation follows the source’s argumentative account rather than silently harmonizing it.

The isolated OCR debris before the printed date carries no recoverable meaning and remains only in the source block.
